<p><em>Duration: 1 h 30 · Session 1 · Associated lab: TP0</em></p>
<h3>1.1 Objectives</h3>
<p>Place the Verilog language within the digital design flow, and install and use the free tool chain.</p>
<h3>1.2 Why a Hardware Description Language (HDL)?</h3>
<p>A modern integrated circuit contains from a few thousand to several billion gates: drawing the schematic
by hand is no longer possible. An HDL lets you <strong>describe</strong> behaviour or structure, <strong>simulate</strong> it
before fabrication, and then automatically <strong>synthesise</strong> it into logic gates (ASIC) or configurable
blocks (FPGA).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>An HDL is <strong>not</strong> a programming language: a Verilog description describes <strong>hardware that
works in parallel</strong>. Every <code>assign</code> and every <code>always</code> block is a piece of circuit that is always "active".</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>1.3 The Design Flow</h3>
<figure><img data-fig="en:flow_conception.svg" alt="Design flow: steps covered by this module (green) and further steps (grey)" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Design flow: steps covered by this module (green) and further steps (grey)</figcaption></figure>

<p>This module covers the first three steps (RTL, simulation, logic synthesis). Place-and-route
and timing analysis are presented only as an outlook.</p>
<h3>1.4 Levels of Abstraction</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Level</th>
<th>Example</th>
<th>Use</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Behavioural</td>
<td><code>always @* y = a + b;</code></td>
<td>Modelling, testbench</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>RTL</strong> (<em>Register Transfer Level</em>)</td>
<td>registers + logic between registers</td>
<td><strong>Synthesis level</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Structural</td>
<td>instances of modules / gates</td>
<td>Hierarchy, netlists</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>1.5 Verilog: First Notions</h3>
<p>A <strong>module</strong> is the basic building block: it has ports (<code>input</code>, <code>output</code>) and a body.</p>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">module half_adder (
    input  a, b,
    output s, c
);
    assign s = a ^ b;     // sum
    assign c = a &amp; b;     // carry
endmodule
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>Verilog is <strong>case-sensitive</strong>; comments: <code>//</code> and <code>/* ... */</code>.</li>
<li><code>assign</code> statements and <code>always</code>/<code>initial</code> blocks execute <strong>in parallel</strong>.</li>
<li>Only a <strong>subset</strong> of the language is synthesisable; the rest (<code>initial</code> with delays, <code>$display</code>, <code>#10</code>…)
  is used for simulation and testbenches.</li>
</ul>
