<li><strong>Always</strong> validate by simulation before synthesising.</li>
<li>Every lab is tested with <code>scripts/run.sh &lt;folder&gt; src</code>: a <code>RESULT: PASS</code> line concludes the simulation.</li>
</ol></section><section class="doc" id="c2"><h2>Chapter 2 — Combinational Logic in Verilog</h2>
<p><em>Duration: 4 h 30 (3 lecture sessions, S2 to S4) · TD1, TP1, TP2</em></p>
<h3>2.1 Data Types</h3>
<ul>
<li><code>wire</code>: a wire, driven by <code>assign</code> or by a module output;</li>
<li><code>reg</code>: a variable assigned in a procedural block (<code>always</code>, <code>initial</code>). <strong><code>reg</code> does not mean "register"</strong>:
  depending on the block, a <code>reg</code> variable becomes either a wire (combinational logic) or a flip-flop (clocked block).</li>
<li>Vectors: <code>wire [7:0] bus;</code> (bit 7 = most significant). Memories: <code>reg [7:0] mem [0:15];</code>.</li>
<li>4-state values: <code>0</code>, <code>1</code>, <code>x</code> (unknown), <code>z</code> (high impedance).</li>
</ul>
<h3>2.2 Literals</h3>
<p><code>&lt;size&gt;'&lt;base&gt;&lt;value&gt;</code>: <code>4'b1010</code>, <code>8'hFF</code>, <code>12'd100</code>, <code>16'sd-5</code> (signed). Without a size: 32 bits (a source of errors!).</p>
<h3>2.3 Operators</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Family</th>
<th>Operators</th>
<th>Remark</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Bitwise</td>
<td><code>~ &amp; \| ^ ~^</code></td>
<td>result has the same width as the operands</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Logical</td>
<td><code>! &amp;&amp; \|\|</code></td>
<td>1-bit result (true/false)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Reduction</td>
<td><code>&amp;x  \|x  ^x  ~&amp;x</code></td>
<td>single operand → 1 bit (e.g. <code>^x</code> = parity)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Arithmetic</td>
<td><code>+ - * / %</code></td>
<td><code>/</code> and <code>%</code> are costly or not synthesisable</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Shift</td>
<td><code>&lt;&lt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;</code></td>
<td><code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> is arithmetic if the operand is signed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Comparison</td>
<td><code>== != &lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;= === !==</code></td>
<td><code>===</code>/<code>!==</code> also compare <code>x</code>/<code>z</code> (testbench)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Concatenation</td>
<td><code>{a, b}</code>; replication <code>{4{a}}</code></td>
<td><code>{8{1'b0}}</code> = 8'h00</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Conditional</td>
<td><code>s ? a : b</code></td>
<td>multiplexer</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>2.3bis Widths: the Rule that Catches Everybody</h3>
<p>In Verilog, the width of an expression is that of the <strong>largest</strong> of its operands <strong>and</strong> of the assignment target.</p>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">wire [7:0] a, b;  wire [8:0] s;
assign s = a + b;            // OK: the addition is done on 9 bits, the carry is kept
wire [7:0] t = a + b;        // carry lost (silent truncation)
assign s = {1'b0, a} + {1'b0, b};   // explicit, recommended
</code></pre>
<h3>2.4 Dataflow Description: <code>assign</code></h3>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">module mux2 #(parameter W = 8) (input [W-1:0] a, b, input s, output [W-1:0] y);
    assign y = s ? b : a;
endmodule
</code></pre>
<h3>2.5 Behavioural Description: <code>always @*</code></h3>
<p>For <code>if</code>/<code>case</code>, we use a procedural block sensitive to <strong>all</strong> its inputs: <code>always @*</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">module dec2to4 (input [1:0] a, input en, output reg [3:0] y);
    always @* begin
        y = 4'b0000;                       // default value
        if (en) case (a)
            2'd0: y = 4'b0001;
            2'd1: y = 4'b0010;
            2'd2: y = 4'b0100;
            2'd3: y = 4'b1000;
        endcase
    end
endmodule
</code></pre>
<p>Rules: <strong>blocking assignments (<code>=</code>)</strong> in a combinational block; every output is assigned a value
<strong>in all cases</strong> (default value at the top of the block, or a final <code>default:</code> / <code>else</code>).</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:rtl_mux4.svg" alt="`mux4` after `proc; opt`: the `case` becomes a parallel multiplexer (`$pmux`) — schematic generated by Yosys" style="width:92%"><figcaption>`mux4` after `proc; opt`: the `case` becomes a parallel multiplexer (`$pmux`) — schematic generated by Yosys</figcaption></figure>

<h3>2.6 The Trap of the Inferred <em>Latch</em></h3>
<p>If a variable is not assigned on every path of an <code>always @*</code> block, the synthesiser must
<strong>remember</strong> the old value: it creates an unwanted <strong>latch</strong>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">always @* if (en) y = a;          // BAD: latch (y is held when en = 0)
always @* begin y = 1'b0; if (en) y = a; end   // GOOD
</code></pre>
<p>Yosys reports the latch (<code>$_DLATCH_</code>), and <code>scripts/synth.sh</code> then prints a warning (TP7).</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:rtl_sel_latch.svg" alt="Code with an incomplete `case`: Yosys instantiates a `$dlatch` (enable cell) — to be avoided" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Code with an incomplete `case`: Yosys instantiates a `$dlatch` (enable cell) — to be avoided</figcaption></figure>

<figure><img data-fig="en:rtl_sel_fixed.svg" alt="After adding `default`: no more latch, just a multiplexer" style="width:92%"><figcaption>After adding `default`: no more latch, just a multiplexer</figcaption></figure>

<h3>2.7 <code>case</code>, <code>casez</code>, Priority</h3>
<p><code>if / else if</code> creates a <strong>priority</strong> (chain), <code>case</code> a parallel <strong>multiplexing</strong>. <code>casez</code> accepts <code>?</code> as a
wildcard (<code>casez (r) 4'b1???: ...</code>). Always finish with <code>default</code>.</p>
<h3>2.8 Functions</h3>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">function [3:0] popcnt8 (input [7:0] x);
    integer i;
    begin popcnt8 = 0; for (i = 0; i &lt; 8; i = i + 1) popcnt8 = popcnt8 + x[i]; end
endfunction
</code></pre>
<p>A <code>function</code> is combinational, with no delay; a <code>for</code> loop is <strong>unrolled</strong> at synthesis.</p>
<h3>2.9 Signed Arithmetic</h3>
<p>Declare <code>signed</code> for two's-complement numbers: <code>wire signed [7:0] a;</code>. Mixing signed and unsigned
in the same expression ⇒ everything is interpreted as <strong>unsigned</strong>. To sign-extend: <code>{{4{a[3]}}, a}</code>.</p>
<h3>2.10 Key Points</h3>
<ol>
<li>A combinational block = <code>always @*</code> + <code>=</code> + <strong>all outputs assigned in all cases</strong>.</li>
<li>Beware of widths and unsized literals.</li>
<li><code>x</code> in simulation = uninitialised signal or conflict: <strong>to be tracked down</strong>.</li>
<li>An inferred latch is almost always a description error.</li>
</ol></section><section class="doc" id="c3"><h2>Chapter 3 — Synchronous Sequential Logic</h2>
<p><em>Duration: 3 h (S5–S6) · TD2, TP3</em></p>
<h3>3.1 The Synchronous Principle</h3>
<p>The entire state of the circuit is stored in <strong>D flip-flops</strong> driven by <strong>one common clock</strong>.
Between two edges, the combinational logic computes the next state; at the edge, it is stored.
Physical constraints: <strong>setup</strong> time (data stable before the edge) and <strong>hold</strong> time (stable after),
verified by static timing analysis (STA), not by functional simulation.</p>
<h3>3.2 The Clocked Block</h3>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">always @(posedge clk) begin
    q &lt;= d;                // NON-blocking assignment
end
</code></pre>
<p>An <code>always @(posedge clk)</code> block describes <strong>flip-flops</strong>: every <code>reg</code> assigned inside it becomes a register.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:timing_setup_hold.svg" alt="Stability window of D around the active edge (&lt;i&gt;t&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;su&lt;/sub&gt;, &lt;i&gt;t&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;h&lt;/sub&gt;) and delay &lt;i&gt;t&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;cq&lt;/sub&gt;" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Stability window of D around the active edge (&lt;i&gt;t&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;su&lt;/sub&gt;, &lt;i&gt;t&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;h&lt;/sub&gt;) and delay &lt;i&gt;t&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;cq&lt;/sub&gt;</figcaption></figure>

<h3>3.3 Reset</h3>
<p><strong>Synchronous</strong> (convention of this course: active high, evaluated at the clock edge):</p>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">always @(posedge clk) if (rst) q &lt;= 8'd0; else q &lt;= d;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Asynchronous</strong> (acts immediately, independently of the clock):</p>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">always @(posedge clk or posedge rst) if (rst) q &lt;= 8'd0; else q &lt;= d;
</code></pre>
<p>Synchronous reset is better suited to FPGAs and timing analysis; asynchronous reset is common in ASICs
(in which case provide a synchronised release). <strong>Never mix the two styles in one project without a reason.</strong></p>
<h3>3.4 Register with Enable</h3>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">always @(posedge clk) if (en) q &lt;= d;     // synthesised: flip-flop with enable ($_DFFE_PP_ in Yosys)
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Never</strong> "gate" the clock by hand (<code>clk &amp; en</code>): use an <em>enable</em>.</p>
<h3>3.5 Blocking vs Non-blocking Assignments — THE Golden Rule</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>Clocked block (sequential)</th>
<th><code>always @*</code> block (combinational)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Operator</td>
<td><strong><code>&lt;=</code></strong> (non-blocking)</td>
<td><strong><code>=</code></strong> (blocking)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A revealing example (3-stage shift register):</p>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">always @(posedge clk) begin q1 &lt;= d; q2 &lt;= q1; q3 &lt;= q2; end   // GOOD: 3 flip-flops in series
always @(posedge clk) begin q1 =  d; q2 =  q1; q3 =  q2; end   // BAD: q3 = d, only one effective flip-flop
</code></pre>
<p>With <code>&lt;=</code>, all the right-hand-side values are read <strong>before</strong> the edge, then all the updates are
applied "at the same time": this is the behaviour of a real register.</p>
<p>Other rules: a <code>reg</code> is assigned in <strong>only one</strong> <code>always</code> block; do not mix <code>=</code> and <code>&lt;=</code> in the same block.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:wave_shift_blocking.svg" alt="Simulation: A (`&lt;=`) is a true shift register, B (`=`) copies `d` everywhere (only one effective flip-flop)" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Simulation: A (`&lt;=`) is a true shift register, B (`=`) copies `d` everywhere (only one effective flip-flop)</figcaption></figure>

<h3>3.6 Classic Counters and Registers</h3>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">module counter #(parameter W = 4) (input clk, rst, en, output reg [W-1:0] q);
    always @(posedge clk) begin
        if (rst)      q &lt;= {W{1'b0}};
        else if (en)  q &lt;= q + 1'b1;          // natural overflow: modulo 2^W
    end
endmodule
</code></pre>
<p>Shift register: <code>sh &lt;= {sh[W-2:0], din};</code>; LFSR: shift + XOR of feedback bits (TP3).</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:rtl_counter.svg" alt="TP3 counter: flip-flop with synchronous reset and enable, priority multiplexers rst &gt; load &gt; en" style="width:92%"><figcaption>TP3 counter: flip-flop with synchronous reset and enable, priority multiplexers rst &gt; load &gt; en</figcaption></figure>

<figure><img data-fig="en:wave_counter.svg" alt="Counter timing diagram (real simulation): pause, count down, load, reset" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Counter timing diagram (real simulation): pause, count down, load, reset</figcaption></figure>

<h3>3.7 Two-block Style: Register + Next-state Logic</h3>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">reg [3:0] state, next;
always @(posedge clk) if (rst) state &lt;= 4'd0; else state &lt;= next;   // sequential
always @*            next = state + 1'b1;                            // combinational
</code></pre>
<p>This split structures the code and will be generalised to FSMs (chapter 4).</p>
<h3>3.8 Asynchronous Signals and Metastability</h3>
<p>An unsynchronised external signal (button, another clock domain) can violate setup/hold and make the
flip-flop <strong>metastable</strong>. The basic remedy: a <strong>two-flip-flop synchroniser</strong>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">reg s1, s2;
always @(posedge clk) begin s1 &lt;= async_in; s2 &lt;= s1; end   // use s2
</code></pre>
<figure><img data-fig="en:synchronizer.svg" alt="Two-flip-flop synchroniser" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Two-flip-flop synchroniser</figcaption></figure>

<h3>3.9 Key Points</h3>
<ol>
<li>Sequential: <code>always @(posedge clk)</code> + <strong><code>&lt;=</code></strong>.</li>
<li>A single, clearly documented reset style.</li>
<li><code>if (en)</code> = enable, no manual clock gating.</li>
<li>Every incoming asynchronous signal is resynchronised.</li>
</ol></section><section class="doc" id="c4"><h2>Chapter 4 — Finite State Machines (FSM)</h2>
<p><em>Duration: 3 h (S7–S8) · TD3, TP4</em></p>
<h3>4.1 Models</h3>
<p>An FSM is defined by a set of states, a <strong>next-state</strong> function and an <strong>output</strong> function.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Moore</strong>: output = f(state). Stable, synchronous output, delayed by one cycle.</li>
<li><strong>Mealy</strong>: output = f(state, inputs). Immediate reaction, but depends on the inputs (risk of glitches, combinational input→output paths).</li>
</ul>
<figure><img data-fig="en:moore_mealy_models.svg" alt="Structure of an FSM: in Mealy the output also depends on the inputs (red arrow)" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Structure of an FSM: in Mealy the output also depends on the inputs (red arrow)</figcaption></figure>

<h3>4.2 Recommended Style: Three Blocks</h3>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">module seq_moore (input clk, rst, in, output out);       // &quot;1011&quot; detector with overlap
    localparam S0 = 3'd0, S1 = 3'd1, S10 = 3'd2, S101 = 3'd3, S1011 = 3'd4;
    reg [2:0] state, next;

    always @(posedge clk)                 // (1) state register
        if (rst) state &lt;= S0; else state &lt;= next;

    always @* begin                       // (2) next state
        next = state;                     //     default value: stay
        case (state)
            S0:    next = in ? S1    : S0;
            S1:    next = in ? S1    : S10;
            S10:   next = in ? S101  : S0;
            S101:  next = in ? S1011 : S10;
            S1011: next = in ? S1    : S10;
            default: next = S0;           //     unused states -&gt; safe return
        endcase
    end

    assign out = (state == S1011);        // (3) output (Moore)
endmodule
</code></pre>
<p>For the <strong>Mealy</strong> version, the output becomes <code>assign out = (state == S101) &amp; in;</code> and the <code>S1011</code> state disappears.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:fsm_moore_1011.svg" alt="Moore state diagram of the 1011 detector" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Moore state diagram of the 1011 detector</figcaption></figure>

<figure><img data-fig="en:fsm_mealy_1011.svg" alt="Mealy diagram: one state fewer, the output is on the transition" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Mealy diagram: one state fewer, the output is on the transition</figcaption></figure>

<figure><img data-fig="en:wave_seq_detector.svg" alt="Simulation: the Moore output (bottom) is delayed by one cycle relative to the Mealy" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Simulation: the Moore output (bottom) is delayed by one cycle relative to the Mealy</figcaption></figure>

<h3>4.3 Design Method</h3>
<ol>
<li>List the states, naming what each one <strong>remembers</strong>.</li>
<li>Draw the diagram (states, transitions labelled with the inputs, outputs).</li>
<li>Check <strong>completeness</strong> (all inputs handled in every state) and <strong>mutual exclusivity</strong> of the transitions.</li>
<li>Code it in three blocks, with <code>default</code> and a default value for <code>next</code>.</li>
<li>Test with a reference model and random sequences.</li>
</ol>
<h3>4.4 State Encoding</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Encoding</th>
<th>Bits for N states</th>
<th>Advantage</th>
<th>Use</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Binary</td>
<td>⌈log₂ N⌉</td>
<td>few flip-flops</td>
<td>small FSMs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><em>One-hot</em></td>
<td>N</td>
<td>simple, fast next-state logic</td>
<td>FPGA, large FSMs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Gray</td>
<td>⌈log₂ N⌉</td>
<td>only 1 bit changes per transition</td>
<td>counters, clock-domain crossing</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Yosys can automatically re-encode an FSM (<code>fsm</code> in <code>synth</code>), provided it detects it.</p>
<h3>4.5 Good Practice</h3>
<ul>
<li>Always provide a <strong>reset</strong> to a defined initial state.</li>
<li>All outputs assigned in all states (default values): no latch.</li>
<li>Name the states with <code>localparam</code>; avoid magic numbers.</li>
<li>If a Mealy output feeds a register in another module, think about the chain of combinational paths.</li>
<li><strong>val/rdy</strong> interfaces (<em>handshake</em>): a transfer takes place when <code>val &amp;&amp; rdy</code> at the clock edge (see GCD, chapter 8).</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.6 Key Points</h3>
<ol>
<li>Moore vs Mealy: one-cycle latency versus dependence on the inputs.</li>
<li>Three blocks: register, next state, outputs.</li>
<li><code>default</code> and default values everywhere.</li>
</ol></section><section class="doc" id="c5"><h2>Chapter 5 — Hierarchy, Parameters and Generation</h2>
<p><em>Duration: 1 h 30 (S9) · TP5</em></p>
<h3>5.1 Module Instantiation</h3>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">adder4 u_add (.a(x), .b(y), .cin(1'b0), .s(sum), .cout(co));   // connection BY NAME (recommended)
</code></pre>
<p>Positional connection (<code>adder4 u (x, y, 1'b0, sum, co)</code>) is fragile: a changed port order silently breaks the circuit.
An unconnected port must be left open explicitly: <code>.cout()</code>. The instance name (<code>u_add</code>) is mandatory.</p>
<h3>5.2 Parameters</h3>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">module counter #(parameter W = 8, parameter MAXV = 255) (...);
counter #(.W(12), .MAXV(4000)) u_c (...);        // override at instantiation
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><code>parameter</code>: can be modified from outside; <code>localparam</code>: internal constant (e.g. <code>localparam AW = $clog2(DEPTH);</code>).</li>
<li><code>$clog2(n)</code>: ⌈log₂ n⌉, useful for sizing counters and addresses.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.3 <code>generate</code></h3>
<p>Repetition or conditional instantiation <strong>at elaboration</strong> (before simulation/synthesis).</p>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">genvar i;
generate
    for (i = 0; i &lt; N; i = i + 1) begin : g_bit        // the block name is mandatory
        fa1 u_fa (.a(a[i]), .b(b[i]), .cin(c[i]), .s(s[i]), .cout(c[i+1]));
    end
endgenerate
</code></pre>
<p><code>generate if (FAST) ... else ...</code> selects an architecture according to a parameter.</p>
<h3>5.4 Memories</h3>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">reg [7:0] mem [0:255];                       // 256 words of 8 bits
always @(posedge clk) if (we) mem[waddr] &lt;= wdata;     // synchronous write
assign rdata = mem[raddr];                   // asynchronous read (or registered for an FPGA RAM)
initial $readmemh(&quot;init.hex&quot;, mem);          // initialisation (simulation / FPGA)
</code></pre>
<p>A large memory is mapped onto the FPGA's dedicated RAM; in an ASIC a memory compiler (<em>macro</em>) is used.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:rtl_adder4.svg" alt="`adder4`: four instances of `full_adder`; the hierarchy is visible on the schematic" style="width:92%"><figcaption>`adder4`: four instances of `full_adder`; the hierarchy is visible on the schematic</figcaption></figure>

<figure><img data-fig="en:schema_fifo.svg" alt="Memory + pointers: example organisation of a FIFO (TP6)" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Memory + pointers: example organisation of a FIFO (TP6)</figcaption></figure>

<h3>5.5 Organising a Project</h3>
<ul>
<li>One module per file, file name = module name;</li>
<li>a top-level module (<em>top</em>) that only <strong>connects</strong>;</li>
<li>separate the <strong>datapath</strong> (registers, operators) from the <strong>control</strong> (FSM) — see chapter 8;</li>
<li><code>src/</code>, <code>tb/</code>, <code>scripts/</code> folders; a <code>Makefile</code> to automate everything.</li>
</ul>
<figure><img data-fig="en:rtl_gcd_top.svg" alt="GCD top-level module: it only connects the control and the datapath" style="width:92%"><figcaption>GCD top-level module: it only connects the control and the datapath</figcaption></figure>

<h3>5.6 Key Points</h3>
<ol>
<li>Instantiate by name, override parameters by name.</li>
<li><code>generate</code> = repetition of <strong>hardware</strong>, not an execution loop.</li>
<li>A clear hierarchy = code that can be tested module by module.</li>
</ol></section><section class="doc" id="c6"><h2>Chapter 6 — Verification: Writing a Good Testbench</h2>
<p><em>Duration: 3 h (S10–S11) · TP5, TP6</em></p>
<h3>6.1 Structure of a Testbench</h3>
<p>A testbench is a module <strong>with no ports</strong> that instantiates the DUT (<em>Device Under Test</em>), generates the stimuli and checks the outputs.</p>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">`timescale 1ns/1ps
module tb;
    reg clk = 0, rst = 1; reg [7:0] d; wire [7:0] q;
    always #5 clk = ~clk;                     // clock with a 10 ns period
    my_reg dut (.clk(clk), .rst(rst), .d(d), .q(q));
    initial begin
        $dumpfile(&quot;tb.vcd&quot;); $dumpvars(0, tb);   // waveforms for GTKWave
        repeat (3) @(negedge clk); rst = 0;
        ...
        $finish;
    end
endmodule
</code></pre>
<h3>6.2 Stimulus Generation and Synchronisation</h3>
<ul>
<li>Delay: <code>#10;</code>; waiting for an event: <code>@(posedge clk)</code>, <code>@(negedge clk)</code>, <code>wait (cond)</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Good <em>race</em>-avoidance practice</strong>: apply the inputs on the <strong>falling</strong> edge (<code>@(negedge clk)</code>) and sample
  on the rising edge: the DUT sees stable inputs at the active edge.</li>
<li>Random: <code>$random</code> (32-bit signed); <code>{$random} % N</code> for a positive integer; seed: <code>$random(seed)</code>.</li>
<li>Files: <code>$readmemh("vectors.hex", tab)</code>.</li>
<li><code>task</code>: a subroutine in which delays are allowed (sending a frame, a transaction…).</li>
</ul>
<h3>6.3 Self-checking</h3>
<p>Inspecting waveforms by eye does <strong>not scale</strong> and cannot be automated. A good testbench:</p>
<ol>
<li>computes the expected result with a <strong>reference model</strong> (a behavioural function, independent of the DUT);</li>
<li>compares with <code>!==</code> (which also detects <code>x</code>/<code>z</code>, unlike <code>!=</code>);</li>
<li>counts the errors and ends with a <strong>machine-readable</strong> verdict:</li>
</ol>
<pre><code class="language-verilog">if (errors == 0) $display(&quot;RESULT: PASS&quot;); else $display(&quot;RESULT: FAIL (%0d errors)&quot;, errors);
$finish;
</code></pre>
<p>The pack's testbenches follow this convention: <code>scripts/run.sh</code> returns code 0 only on PASS.</p>
<h3>6.4 <em>Scoreboard</em> and Random Verification</h3>
<p>The <strong>scoreboard</strong> is a reference model updated in parallel with the DUT and compared on every cycle
(TP6: reference FIFO as a circular array). Combine:
* <strong>directed tests</strong>: corner cases (full, empty, 0, maximum value, overflow);
* <strong>random tests</strong>: a large number of cycles, several profiles (frequent writes, frequent reads, bursts).</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:wave_fifo.svg" alt="Excerpt from the random FIFO testbench simulation: random writes/reads, `full`/`empty` flags" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Excerpt from the random FIFO testbench simulation: random writes/reads, `full`/`empty` flags</figcaption></figure>

<h3>6.5 Verification Quality: Mutation Testing</h3>
<p>How do you know whether a testbench is any good? You deliberately inject a <strong>bug</strong> (a mutant) into the DUT;
a good testbench must <strong>fail</strong>. TP6 provides <code>mutant/fifo_sync.v</code> (a FIFO declared full one word too early):
<code>scripts/run.sh 03_tp/TP6_testbench_fifo mutant</code> must print <code>FAIL</code>.</p>
<h3>6.6 Coverage (outlook)</h3>
<p>Line, branch, FSM-state and functional coverage. Verilator and commercial simulators provide
coverage tools; the idea: measure what the tests have actually exercised.</p>
<h3>6.7 Classic Testbench Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Forgetting <code>$finish</code> → endless simulation (the clock never stops); provide a <strong>watchdog</strong> (<code>timeout</code>).</li>
<li>Comparing with <code>!=</code> instead of <code>!==</code>: an <code>x</code> goes unnoticed.</li>
<li>Driving the DUT on the active edge: <em>race condition</em>.</li>
<li>A reference model copied from the DUT: it reproduces the same errors; write it <strong>independently</strong> (another algorithm, from the spec).</li>
</ul>
<h3>6.8 Key Points</h3>
<ol>
<li>Testbench = DUT + stimuli + reference model + automatic verdict.</li>
<li>Inputs on <code>negedge</code>, observation after the edge.</li>
<li>Evaluate the testbench by mutation.</li>
</ol></section><section class="doc" id="c7"><h2>Chapter 7 — Logic Synthesis with Yosys</h2>
<p><em>Duration: 1 h 30 (S12) · TP7, TD4</em></p>
<h3>7.1 What Is Synthesis?</h3>
<p>Transforming an RTL description into an equivalent <strong>network of gates</strong> (netlist), optimised for a target
(FPGA or ASIC cell library). <strong>Yosys</strong> is an open-source synthesiser; its <code>synth</code> script chains
together elementary passes.</p>
<h3>7.2 The Main Passes of <code>synth -top &lt;module&gt;</code></h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Pass</th>
<th>Role</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>hierarchy</code></td>
<td>builds the hierarchy from the <em>top</em> module, removes unused modules</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>proc</code></td>
<td>converts <code>always</code>/<code>if</code>/<code>case</code> blocks into multiplexers and flip-flops; <strong>detects latches</strong> (<code>proc_dlatch</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>opt</code> / <code>opt_expr</code></td>
<td>logic simplifications, constant propagation, removal of dead logic</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>fsm</code></td>
<td>detects and re-encodes state machines</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>memory</code></td>
<td>infers memories</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>techmap</code></td>
<td>replaces operators (<code>+</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, …) with elementary gates</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>abc</code></td>
<td>optimises and maps the logic onto a set of generic gates</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>stat</code></td>
<td>prints the statistics (cells, wires, hierarchy)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Command used in this module:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">yosys -p &quot;read_verilog gcd.v; synth -top gcd; stat&quot;
# or, with our script: scripts/synth.sh gcd file1.v file2.v
</code></pre>
<h3>7.3 Reading a <code>stat</code> Report</h3>
<p>Each <code>Number of cells</code> line is followed by the breakdown by <strong>cell type</strong>. Yosys's generic logic cells:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Cell</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>$_AND_</code>, <code>$_OR_</code>, <code>$_XOR_</code>, <code>$_XNOR_</code>, <code>$_NOT_</code>, <code>$_NAND_</code>, <code>$_NOR_</code></td>
<td>2-input gates (1 input for NOT)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>$_ANDNOT_</code>, <code>$_ORNOT_</code></td>
<td><code>A &amp; ~B</code>, <code>A \| ~B</code> (gates with one inverted input)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>$_MUX_</code></td>
<td>2-to-1 multiplexer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>$_DFF_P_</code></td>
<td>D flip-flop, rising edge</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>$_DFFE_PP_</code></td>
<td>D flip-flop with <strong>enable</strong> (first P = clock on rising edge, second P = active-high enable)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>$_SDFF_PP0_</code></td>
<td>flip-flop with <strong>synchronous reset</strong> (clk P, active-high reset, reset value <strong>0</strong>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>$_DLATCH_P_</code></td>
<td><strong>latch</strong>: almost always a bug</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>With <code>-top</code>, the report is presented <strong>per module</strong>, followed by a <strong>design hierarchy</strong> section giving the cumulative total
over the whole hierarchy (number of instances × content).</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Warning</strong>: <code>stat</code> without a library counts <em>generic</em> cells, not mm² or nanoseconds.
For real area, you supply a <code>.lib</code> library (<code>stat -liberty cells.lib</code>); for critical-path
time, you use an STA tool (for example OpenSTA). The number of cells is a useful <strong>relative
indicator</strong> for comparing two architectures.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>7.4 Example: "16 bits equal to zero"</h3>
<p>The case-study report (chapter 8) gives for a 16-bit <code>ZeroComparator</code>: <strong>15 cells</strong>
(3 <code>$_ANDNOT_</code>, 1 <code>$_NOR_</code>, 11 <code>$_OR_</code>). It is a reduction tree: a 16-bit OR requires <strong>15 2-input
gates</strong> (n − 1). When interpreting a result, always relate it to the expected structure.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:gate_zero_cmp.svg" alt="16-bit `ZeroComparator` after synthesis: a tree of 15 2-input gates (n − 1)" style="width:92%"><figcaption>16-bit `ZeroComparator` after synthesis: a tree of 15 2-input gates (n − 1)</figcaption></figure>

<h3>7.5 Errors Detected at Synthesis</h3>
<ul>
<li><em>Inferred latch</em> (incomplete <code>if</code>/<code>case</code>) → <code>$_DLATCH_</code> in <code>stat</code>; <code>Latch inferred</code> message in the log.</li>
<li>Inconsistent bus width (<code>Resizing</code> warnings, <code>Warning: ...  width</code>).</li>
<li>Signal never driven (<code>undriven</code>), combinational loop.</li>
<li><code>initial</code> with a value: accepted for FPGAs only; avoid in generic RTL.</li>
</ul>
<h3>7.6 Post-synthesis Simulation</h3>
<pre><code class="language-bash">yosys -p &quot;read_verilog gcd.v; synth -top gcd; write_verilog -noattr gcd_syn.v&quot;
iverilog -g2005 -o gl.vvp tb_gcd.v gcd_syn.v &amp;&amp; vvp gl.vvp      # same testbench, gate-level netlist
</code></pre>
<p>The same testbench must pass on the netlist: this is an <strong>equivalence check by simulation</strong>
(formal proof, which is more rigorous, is outside the syllabus).</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:chart_additionneurs_vs_largeur.svg" alt="Cost of an adder depending on how it is written (Yosys measurements): little difference, but the number of cells says nothing about computation time" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Cost of an adder depending on how it is written (Yosys measurements): little difference, but the number of cells says nothing about computation time</figcaption></figure>

<figure><img data-fig="en:chart_alu_cout_operations.svg" alt="Cost of each operation of an 8-bit ALU: addition/subtraction dominates, and the full ALU costs more than the sum of its parts (output multiplexing, two separate adders)" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Cost of each operation of an 8-bit ALU: addition/subtraction dominates, and the full ALU costs more than the sum of its parts (output multiplexing, two separate adders)</figcaption></figure>

<h3>7.7 Key Points</h3>
<ol>
<li><code>proc</code> → <code>opt</code> → <code>fsm</code>/<code>memory</code> → <code>techmap</code> → <code>abc</code> → <code>stat</code>.</li>
<li>Reading a report: flip-flop types, number of cells per module, hierarchy.</li>
<li>Zero latches in clean synchronous code.</li>
</ol></section><section class="doc" id="c8"><h2>Chapter 8 — Case Study: the GCD Computation Unit</h2>
<p><em>Duration: 1 h 30 (S13) · TD4, TP8 (project)</em></p>
<h3>8.1 Specification</h3>
<p>Compute the greatest common divisor of two 16-bit integers <strong>A</strong> and <strong>B</strong> using Euclid's algorithm
(subtractions and swaps), with a <strong>handshake</strong> interface:</p>
<ul>
<li>request: <code>req_val</code>, <code>req_rdy</code>, <code>req_msg[31:0] = {A[15:0], B[15:0]}</code>;</li>
<li>response: <code>resp_val</code>, <code>resp_rdy</code>, <code>resp_msg[15:0]</code>;</li>
<li><code>clk</code>, <code>reset</code>: in total <strong>8 ports, 54 bits</strong> (1+1+32+1+1+16+1+1).</li>
</ul>
<p>A transfer takes place at a clock edge where <code>val</code> and <code>rdy</code> are both 1.</p>
<h3>8.2 Algorithm</h3>
<pre><code>while B ≠ 0:
    if A &lt; B: swap A and B
    else    : A ← A − B
result: A
</code></pre>
<p>Corner cases: gcd(a, 0) = a; gcd(0, b) = b (one swap is enough); gcd(0, 0) = 0.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:gcd_algo.svg" alt="Flowchart of the algorithm" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Flowchart of the algorithm</figcaption></figure>

<h3>8.3 Control / Datapath Architecture</h3>
<figure><img data-fig="en:archi_gcd.svg" alt="GCD architecture: datapath (blue) and control (red, dotted)" style="width:92%"><figcaption>GCD architecture: datapath (blue) and control (red, dotted)</figcaption></figure>

<ul>
<li><strong>Datapath</strong>: 2 16-bit registers with enable (A, B), a 3-way multiplexer (input / A−B / B)
  for A, a 2-way multiplexer (input / A) for B, a subtractor, an <code>A&lt;B</code> comparator, a <code>B=0</code> detector.</li>
<li><strong>Controller</strong>: 3-state FSM (<strong>2-bit</strong> state register with synchronous reset).</li>
</ul>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>State</th>
<th>Outputs</th>
<th>Transition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>IDLE</td>
<td><code>req_rdy=1</code>, loads A and B from <code>req_msg</code></td>
<td><code>req_val</code> → CALC</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CALC</td>
<td>if <code>B≠0</code>: swap (if <code>A&lt;B</code>) otherwise <code>A←A−B</code></td>
<td><code>B=0</code> → DONE</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>DONE</td>
<td><code>resp_val=1</code>, <code>resp_msg = A</code></td>
<td><code>resp_rdy</code> → IDLE</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figure><img data-fig="en:fsm_gcd.svg" alt="Controller FSM" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Controller FSM</figcaption></figure>

<h3>8.4 Verification</h3>
<p><code>tb_gcd.v</code>: Euclid reference function (modulo), corner cases (<code>(0,0)</code>, <code>(a,0)</code>, <code>(0,b)</code>, <code>(65535,1)</code>…),
150 random cases and <strong>random back-pressure</strong> on <code>resp_rdy</code>; check that <code>resp_val</code> is held as long as
<code>resp_rdy</code> is low.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:wave_gcd.svg" alt="Simulation of gcd(18, 12): states IDLE → CALC → DONE; A and B go through 18/12 → 6/12 → 12/6 → 6/6 → 0/6 → 6/0" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Simulation of gcd(18, 12): states IDLE → CALC → DONE; A and B go through 18/12 → 6/12 → 12/6 → 6/6 → 0/6 → 6/0</figcaption></figure>

