<h3>8.6 Optimisation Ideas (project, bonus)</h3>
<ol>
<li>Remove the comparator by reusing the sign bit of the subtraction (<code>A − B</code> on 17 bits).</li>
<li>Replace the swap with a computation of <code>|A − B|</code> (Stein's binary algorithm): shifts instead of repeated subtractions.</li>
<li>Compare the <code>stat</code> reports before/after; check that <code>tb_gcd</code> still passes.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Latency: a computation whose duration depends on the data</h4>
<p>Each subtraction or swap costs <strong>one cycle</strong>. For two random 16-bit numbers the typical latency is about 50 cycles, but
the worst case <code>gcd(65535, 1)</code> requires 65,535 operations. The chart below is based on <strong>20,000 pairs</strong> with a model validated
on 400 RTL simulations (constant offset of 1 cycle for the handshake).</p>
