  $_NOT_                      1      $_XNOR_           3
  $_ORNOT_                    8      $_XOR_           15
</code></pre>
<ol>
<li>How many flip-flops does the report show? How many were expected from the registers in the code? (1 pt)</li>
<li>Explain the difference (hint: look at the values taken by the most significant bit of <code>sh</code>). (1 pt)</li>
<li>Decode <code>$_SDFFE_PP1P_</code>. Which register in the code corresponds to this type, and why? (1 pt)</li>
</ol>
<h3>A3 — Sizing (3 pts)</h3>
<p>The clock is 50 MHz and the serial link is 115,200 baud (10-bit frame).
1. Compute <code>CPB</code> (nearest integer) and the relative error on the baud rate. (1 pt)
2. What is the minimum width of the counter <code>cnt</code> (with <code>$clog2</code>)? Note that the part B code reserves 16 bits: what is the cost? (1 pt)
3. Duration of a frame, in µs and in clock cycles? (1 pt)</p>
<h2>Part B — 8N1 UART Transmitter (12 pts)</h2>
<p>File to complete: <code>04_examens/EXAMEN_FINAL/etudiant/uart_tx.v</code>. Test: <code>scripts/run.sh 04_examens/EXAMEN_FINAL etudiant</code>.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:wave_uart_frame.svg" alt="Example frame for `data = 8&#x27;hA3`, `CPB = 4`" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Example frame for `data = 8&#x27;hA3`, `CPB = 4`</figcaption></figure>

<p><strong>Specification.</strong> <code>uart_tx #(CPB)</code>: inputs <code>clk</code>, <code>rst</code> (synchronous, active high), <code>start</code>, <code>data[7:0]</code>; outputs <code>tx</code>, <code>busy</code>.</p>
<ul>
<li>At idle, <code>tx = 1</code> and <code>busy = 0</code>.</li>
<li>If <code>start = 1</code> is sampled at a clock edge while <code>busy = 0</code>, <code>data</code> is stored and the frame is sent:
  <strong>start</strong> bit (0), <code>data[0]</code> … <code>data[7]</code> (least significant bit first), <strong>stop</strong> bit (1).</li>
<li>Each bit lasts exactly <code>CPB</code> cycles. Just after the edge that sampled <code>start</code>, <code>tx</code> is already 0 (output coming from a register).</li>
<li><code>busy = 1</code> during the whole frame (10 × <code>CPB</code> cycles) and then drops to 0. A <code>start</code> received while <code>busy = 1</code> is <strong>ignored</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>To hand in</strong>: <code>uart_tx.v</code>; the result of <code>scripts/synth.sh uart_tx etudiant/uart_tx.v</code> (number of cells, absence of latches).</p>
<p><strong>Part B marking</strong>: idle 1 pt · normal frames 5 pts · <code>start</code> ignored while <code>busy</code> 2 pts · quality 4 pts (synchronous reset,
<code>&lt;=</code> in sequential logic, no latch at synthesis, <code>CPB</code> truly parameterisable, readable code).</p></section><section class="doc" id="resit"><h2>Resit Session — Digital Design and Verilog HDL</h2>
<p><em>Duration: 1 h 30 · Handout allowed · Computer: iverilog/yosys · /20</em></p>
<p><strong>Instructions.</strong> Files in <code>04_examens/RATTRAPAGE/etudiant/</code>. Test: <code>scripts/run.sh 04_examens/RATTRAPAGE etudiant</code>
(<code>OK</code>/<code>KO</code> for each exercise). Synchronous reset, active high; no latch.</p>
<h2>Part A — Theory and Analysis (6 pts)</h2>
<p><strong>A1 (2 pts)</strong> (1) Explain why a <code>case</code> without <code>default</code> in an <code>always @*</code> can produce a latch. (2) What is a mutation test?</p>
<p><strong>A2 (4 pts)</strong> Report <code>synth -top debounce; stat</code> for the module of exercise 3 of part B (<code>N = 4</code>):</p>
<pre><code>Number of cells:            73
  $_ANDNOT_ 10   $_AND_ 2   $_NAND_ 6   $_NOT_ 1   $_ORNOT_ 1   $_OR_ 20
  $_SDFFE_PP0P_ 1   $_SDFF_PP0_ 16   $_XNOR_ 7   $_XOR_ 9
</code></pre>
<ol>
<li>How many flip-flops? Relate them to the registers in the code (<code>dout</code>, <code>cnt</code> declared on 16 bits in this reference version). (1 pt)</li>
<li>Why is this number disproportionate for <code>N = 4</code>? What width is sufficient, and how many flip-flops in total? (2 pts)</li>
<li>Which cell type corresponds to <code>dout</code>, and why does it have an enable whereas <code>cnt</code> does not? (1 pt)</li>
</ol>
<h2>Part B — Design (14 pts)</h2>
<p><strong>Exercise 1 — Gray Code (4 pts)</strong> — <code>ex1_gray.v</code>. <code>bin2gray #(W)</code>: <code>g = b ^ (b &gt;&gt; 1)</code>. <code>gray2bin #(W)</code>: <code>b[W-1] = g[W-1]</code>, <code>b[i] = b[i+1] ^ g[i]</code> (<code>for</code> loop in <code>always @*</code>).</p>
<p><strong>Exercise 2 — 4-bit PWM (4 pts)</strong> — <code>ex2_pwm.v</code>. Free-running 4-bit counter (synchronous reset to 0); <code>out = (counter &lt; duty)</code>, combinational output. Over 16 cycles <code>out</code> is 1 for <code>duty</code> cycles.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:wave_pwm.svg" alt="Exercise 2: expected behaviour for `duty` = 4, 8 then 12" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Exercise 2: expected behaviour for `duty` = 4, 8 then 12</figcaption></figure>

<p><strong>Exercise 3 — Debouncer (6 pts)</strong> — <code>ex3_debounce.v</code>. <code>debounce #(N = 4)</code>: <code>dout</code> (register, 0 after reset) copies <code>din</code> only if <code>din</code> has remained <strong>different from <code>dout</code></strong> for <code>N</code> consecutive clock edges. As soon as <code>din = dout</code>, the count restarts from zero. Build the counter with a width suited to <code>N</code> (<code>$clog2</code>).</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:wave_debounce.svg" alt="Exercise 3: expected behaviour (N = 4) — bounces shorter than 4 cycles do not change `dout`" style="width:92%"><figcaption>Exercise 3: expected behaviour (N = 4) — bounces shorter than 4 cycles do not change `dout`</figcaption></figure></section><section class="doc" id="f-readme"><h2>FPGA Lab Work on the iCEBreaker Board 🎓</h2>
