<h3>3. Programming</h3>
<ul>
<li><code>fpga.sh prog</code>: <strong>volatile</strong> configuration (SRAM): fast, ideal in lab sessions; the board forgets everything at power-off.</li>
<li><code>fpga.sh flash</code>: writes the flash memory: the circuit restarts by itself.</li>
<li><strong>USB permissions (Linux)</strong>: if <code>iceprog</code> answers "Can't find iCE FTDI USB device", create the following udev rule, then unplug and replug:</li>
</ul>
<pre><code class="language-bash">echo 'ACTION==&quot;add&quot;, ATTRS{idVendor}==&quot;0403&quot;, ATTRS{idProduct}==&quot;6010&quot;, MODE:=&quot;666&quot;' | sudo tee /etc/udev/rules.d/53-lattice-ftdi.rules
sudo udevadm control --reload-rules &amp;&amp; sudo udevadm trigger
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><strong>WSL2</strong>: the USB port is not visible by default: use <code>usbipd</code> (on the Windows side) to attach the board.</li>
<li>The FTDI chip exposes two channels: one is used for programming, <strong>the other for the serial port</strong> (often <code>/dev/ttyUSB1</code>).
  Terminal: <code>screen /dev/ttyUSB1 115200</code> (quit: Ctrl-A then K); or <code>scripts/serial_check.py /dev/ttyUSB1</code> for lab F3.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. FPGA Particularities for the Student Coming from Simulation</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>No reset on the board</strong>: registers receive their value <strong>at configuration</strong>: initialise them at their declaration
   (<code>reg [7:0] r = 0;</code>). This is accepted by Yosys for the iCE40; it is not portable to all devices.</li>
<li><strong>An FPGA is not a processor</strong>: an "always active" module consumes resources permanently.</li>
<li><strong>Time is counted in cycles</strong>: 12 MHz → 83.3 ns per cycle; 1 s = 12,000,000 cycles (24-bit counter).</li>
<li><strong>Simulation is not enough</strong>: a correct description can be too slow (Fmax) or too big; only place-and-route tells you.</li>
<li><strong>Specialised resources</strong> (BRAM, DSP, PLL) are obtained through a specific coding style or by instantiation.</li>
</ol>
