   the ROM by a giant 256-entry <code>case</code>, or compare with the utilisation in the report.)</li>
</ol>
<h3>5. Going Further</h3>
<p>Replace the triangle with a tabulated sine (ROM of 256 values) for a more natural "heartbeat" effect.</p></section><section class="doc" id="f3"><h2>F3 — Serial Terminal over USB: UART Receiver</h2>
<p><em>Duration: 3 h · File: <code>src/uart_rx.v</code> (to complete); <code>common/uart_tx.v</code> and <code>src/top_f3.v</code> supplied</em></p>
<h3>Objectives</h3>
<p>Design a robust <strong>asynchronous serial receiver</strong>: resynchronise, detect the start of a frame, <strong>sample in the middle of each bit</strong>,
report framing errors; test it from the PC side.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:wave_f3_uart_rx.svg" alt="Reception of 0xA3: each bit is sampled in its middle" style="width:100%"><figcaption>Reception of 0xA3: each bit is sampled in its middle</figcaption></figure>

<h3>1. 8N1 Format</h3>
<p>Idle at 1; <strong>start</strong> bit (0); 8 data bits, <strong>least significant first</strong>; <strong>stop</strong> bit (1). Baud rate 115,200 with a 12 MHz clock:
<code>CPB = 12,000,000 / 115,200 ≈ 104</code> cycles per bit.</p>
<h3>2. Work to Do</h3>
<p>Write <code>uart_rx #(CPB)</code>: asynchronous input <code>rx</code> (two resynchronisation flip-flops), outputs <code>data[7:0]</code>, <code>valid</code> (one-cycle pulse)
and <code>frame_err</code> (simultaneous pulse if the stop bit is 0).</p>
<p>Algorithm: on a falling edge, wait <code>CPB/2</code> cycles and <strong>confirm</strong> that the line is still at 0 (otherwise: glitch, return to idle);
then sample every <code>CPB</code> cycles (middle of each bit); check the stop bit.</p>
<p>The module <code>top_f3</code> (supplied) <strong>echoes</strong> every byte, inverts LED <code>i</code> on receiving the character <code>'1'</code>..<code>'5'</code>, answers <code>'?'</code> with the state of the
5 LEDs (5 digits + line feed) and lights the red LED on a framing error.</p>
<h3>3. Validate</h3>
<pre><code class="language-bash">scripts/fpga.sh sim F3_uart_rx src              # includes clock offset, glitch, framing error, echo
scripts/fpga.sh all F3_uart_rx src
scripts/fpga.sh prog F3_uart_rx src
screen /dev/ttyUSB1 115200                      # type characters; Ctrl-A then K to quit
scripts/serial_check.py /dev/ttyUSB1            # automatic test from the PC side
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Check on the board</strong>: [ ] correct echo of any character [ ] <code>3</code> inverts LED[2] [ ] <code>?</code> returns 5 digits then a line feed [ ] <code>serial_check.py</code>: TEST SÉRIE OK (the script prints this in French: "serial test OK").</p>
<h3>4. Questions</h3>
<ol>
<li>What is the actual baud rate obtained with <code>CPB = 104</code>? What relative error?</li>
<li>Why sample in the <strong>middle</strong> of the bit? What maximum clock error is tolerated over a 10-bit frame? (Compute.)</li>
<li>What happens if the two resynchronisation flip-flops are forgotten? (State the risk, not just the principle.)</li>
<li>Why confirm the start bit in the middle? What would the receiver do without this precaution when faced with a glitch of a few cycles?</li>
<li>In <code>top_f3</code>, why does the transmit sequencer wait for <code>!txb</code>? What would happen if characters arrived faster than they can be sent back out?</li>
</ol></section><section class="doc" id="f4"><h2>F4 — Timing Closure: Maximum Frequency, PLL and DSP Block</h2>
<p><em>Duration: 3 h · Files: <code>src/mac16.v</code> (starting point), <code>src/pll48.v</code> and <code>src/top_f4.v</code> supplied, <code>exemples/mac16_lut_pipeline.v</code>, <code>lab.cfg</code></em></p>
<h3>Objectives</h3>
<p>Observe that a description that is <strong>correct in simulation</strong> can be <strong>unusable</strong> at the target frequency; read a <strong>critical path</strong>;
compare three implementations of a multiplier-accumulator; use a <strong>PLL</strong> and a <strong>DSP block</strong>.</p>
<h3>1. The Circuit</h3>
<p><code>top_f4</code> generates 12 MHz → <strong>48 MHz</strong> with the iCE40 PLL (<code>pll48.v</code>, parameters computed by <code>icepll -i 12 -o 48</code>), feeds a
<strong>16×16 unsigned MAC → 40-bit accumulator</strong> with pseudo-random data (32-bit LFSR) and shows the top of the accumulator on the LEDs.
The green LED indicates that the PLL is locked. Goal: <strong>48 MHz</strong> (<code>FREQ=48</code> in <code>lab.cfg</code>).</p>
<p>The PLL formula: <code>f_out = f_in × (DIVF + 1) / (2^DIVQ × (DIVR + 1))</code>; with DIVR=0, DIVF=63, DIVQ=4: <code>12 × 64 / 16 = 48 MHz</code>.</p>
<h3>2. Part 1 — Measure the Starting Point</h3>
<pre><code class="language-bash">scripts/fpga.sh sim F4_timing_pll_dsp src        # the naive MAC is FUNCTIONALLY correct: PASS
