<p><em>Duration: 3 h · File: <code>src/sequencer.v</code> (to complete), <code>src/top_f5.v</code> supplied</em></p>
<h3>Objectives</h3>
<p>Use a <strong>synchronous RAM</strong> (write + read) inferred as BRAM; handle its <strong>one-cycle read latency</strong> in an FSM;
design a state-based controller with command pulses.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:wave_f5_sequenceur.svg" alt="Recording of 4 patterns then looped playback" style="width:100%"><figcaption>Recording of 4 patterns then looped playback</figcaption></figure>

<h3>1. Operation</h3>
<p>The sequencer stores up to 32 "patterns" (<code>W</code>-bit values) taken from <code>pattern_in</code> on each <code>tick</code> pulse, then replays them
in a loop. On the board (<code>W = 3</code>): the three Pmod buttons build a 3-bit pattern; the on-board button advances the cycle
<strong>record → stop → play → stop</strong>. <code>LED[3]</code> = recording, <code>LED[4]</code> = playback.</p>
<h3>2. Work to Do (<code>src/sequencer.v</code>)</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong><code>pattern_ram</code></strong>: 32 words, synchronous write and read <strong>in the same <code>always</code> block</strong> (for BRAM inference); read with 1 cycle of latency.</li>
<li><strong><code>sequencer</code></strong>: three states <code>IDLE / REC / PLAY</code> (full specification in a comment in the file). Delicate points: the data read
   is available <strong>only one cycle after</strong> the address; the recorded length; the return to address 0 after the last pattern; the return to <code>IDLE</code> after the 32nd.</li>
</ol>
<h3>3. Validate</h3>
<pre><code class="language-bash">scripts/fpga.sh sim F5_ram_sequenceur src       # independent reference model, 12,000 random cycles
scripts/fpga.sh all F5_ram_sequenceur src       # requires 1 SB_RAM40_4K
scripts/fpga.sh prog F5_ram_sequenceur src
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Check on the board</strong>: [ ] record 4 patterns (3 buttons), stop, play back: the sequence loops [ ] a new recording replaces the old one
[ ] playing with no recorded pattern does nothing.</p>
<h3>4. Questions</h3>
<ol>
<li>Why does the specification forbid a <code>tick</code> in the 2 cycles following <code>rec</code> or <code>play</code>? What bug would appear otherwise?</li>
<li>How many bits are stored? What fraction of the BRAM is used (one SB_RAM40_4K = 4 kbit)?</li>
<li>The iCE40 has no LUT-based "distributed" RAM: what would the same memory cost in flip-flops? (Check by replacing <code>mem</code> with registers.)</li>
<li>How would you add a 5th "clear" button without modifying <code>pattern_ram</code>?</li>
<li>What would happen if the same address were read and written in the same cycle? (Read the BRAM documentation.)</li>
</ol></section><section class="doc" id="f6"><h2>F6 — Project: Reflex Game</h2>
<p><em>Duration: 6 h (2 sessions) · Files: <code>src/reflex.v</code> (to complete), <code>src/top_f6.v</code> supplied, <code>exemples/msg_tx_division.v</code></em></p>
