<figure><img data-fig="en:archi_f6.svg" alt="Project architecture" style="width:100%"><figcaption>Project architecture</figcaption></figure>

<h3>1. Rules of the Game</h3>
<p>Pressing <strong>BTN[0]</strong> starts a round: a <strong>random</strong> wait begins (LED[2]). Then LED[0] lights up: you must press <strong>BTN[1]</strong>
as fast as possible. The reaction time in milliseconds is sent over the serial port (<code>"0245 ms"</code>). Pressing <strong>too early</strong>: "false start"
(LED[1] lit, message <code>FAUX</code>, French for "false"). If nobody presses within 9.999 s, the result is 9999.</p>
<figure><img data-fig="en:fsm_reflexes.svg" alt="Referee FSM" style="width:100%"><figcaption>Referee FSM</figcaption></figure>

<h3>2. Work to Do (<code>src/reflex.v</code>)</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong><code>reflex_game</code></strong>: the FSM above. Wait delay = <code>500 + rnd</code> ms (<code>rnd</code> on 12 bits). The counter <code>t</code> counts the <code>tick</code>s (1 per ms);
   result = <code>t</code> at the press; one-cycle <code>done</code> and <code>err</code> pulses; <code>start</code> ignored in WAIT/GO, <code>btn</code> ignored in IDLE/RES/ERR.</li>
<li><strong><code>msg_tx</code></strong>: sends <code>"NNNN ms\r\n"</code> (4 digits, leading zeros) or <code>"FAUX\r\n"</code> byte by byte via <code>tx_start / tx_data / tx_busy</code>
   (wait for <code>tx_busy</code> to rise then fall between two bytes). <strong>Do not use combinational <code>/</code> and <code>%</code></strong>: convert by successive
   subtractions (see part 4).</li>
</ol>
<h3>3. Validate</h3>
<pre><code class="language-bash">scripts/fpga.sh sim F6_projet_reflexes src
scripts/fpga.sh all F6_projet_reflexes src
scripts/fpga.sh prog F6_projet_reflexes src
screen /dev/ttyUSB1 115200
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Check on the board</strong>: [ ] the message appears after each round [ ] typical time 200–300 ms [ ] early press: <code>FAUX</code> and LED[1] [ ] the wait delay
varies from one round to the next [ ] a press of BTN[0] during the round has no effect.</p>
<h3>4. The Division Trap</h3>
<p>Replace <code>msg_tx</code> with the version from <code>exemples/msg_tx_division.v</code> (which writes <code>v/1000</code>, <code>(v/100)%10</code>…). The simulation still passes.
